  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">PREVENTING A LETHAL BACTERIAL DISEASE IN THE CHIMPANZEES OF SIERRA LEONE</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE PURPOSE OF THIS PROJECT IS TO GENERATE CRITICAL INFORMATION ON THE ECOLOGY AND BIOLOGY OF A BACTERIUM RESPONSIBLE FOR AN EMERGING AND POSSIBLY ZOONOTIC DISEASE (EPIZOOTIC NEUROLOGIC AND GASTROENTERIC SYNDROME, ENGS) KILLING CRITICALLY ENDANGERED CHIMPANZEES AT A SANCTUARY IN SIERRA LEONE, AND TO ASSESS THE THREAT TO WILD APES BY: (1) CONDUCTING ECOLOGICAL STUDIES TO DETERMINE THE SOURCE OF INFECTION, EXPLAIN SEASONAL PATTERNS OF MORTALITY, AND IDENTIFY CRITICAL MANAGEMENT-RELATED INTERVENTIONS FOR STOPPING THE SPREAD OF THE DISEASE (2) DETERMINING HOW WIDESPREAD THE BACTERIUM AND SYNDROME ARE IN EQUATORIAL AFRICAN SANCTUARIES AND IN THE WILD AND (3) DETERMINING OPTIMAL GROWTH CONDITIONS FOR THE BACTERIUM IN THE LABORATORY TO FACILITATE FUTURE INVESTIGATIONS OF TREATMENTS AND PREVENTIONS.</narrative>
  </description>
